# Cron ticks the pre-push deploy guard refuses to deploy on top of.
#
# THE SINGLE DECLARED SOURCE for scripts/hooks/pre-push. The hook derives
# its tick times from this file; it hardcodes nothing. tests/
# test_pre_push_hook.py reads this same file and drives the hook at times
# computed from it, so the two cannot silently disagree.
#
# These MUST mirror the Railway cron configuration, which is the real
# source of truth and lives on each service instance (Railway dashboard,
# or `railway api` -> environment.serviceInstances[].cronSchedule). They
# are NOT read from Railway at push time on purpose: a git hook that
# needs a network call to a third party fails when you are offline, and
# the repo forbids tests that touch the network, so nothing could verify
# it either.
#
# WHEN A SCHEDULE CHANGES IN RAILWAY, CHANGE IT HERE IN THE SAME COMMIT.
# Getting this wrong is not theoretical: v1.193.0 moved pricing from
# `0 6,14,22` to `25 1,6,11,16,21` and left the hook guarding the old
# times for a day -- it refused pushes at 14:00 UTC when nothing ran, and
# waved them through at 11:25 UTC when pricing was live.
#
# Format: <minute> <hours>  <service name>
# Only the two fields the guard needs are parsed. Hours may be a single
# value or a comma-separated list; `*` is not supported and is refused
# loudly, because an hourly job is a different question (see the
# CHANGELOG for v1.194.1 on why order-sync and colour-backfill are not
# guarded here).
#
# ONLY jobs whose real work runs INSIDE the app container belong here --
# those are the ones a deploy kills mid-flight. A job that runs in its own
# Railway cron service and merely calls the app over HTTP does not.

# Flow B pricing preview: background task inside the app container.
25 1,6,11,16,21   cardfoundry-cron-pricing

# Perform Sync: runs synchronously inside one HTTP request.
30 2,10,18        cardfoundry-cron-perform-sync
